# Git stores text as LF, but core.autocrlf=true on Windows converts it to
# CRLF on checkout. Prettier's endOfLine defaults to lf, so a fresh clone
# would fail `npx prettier --check src/` on files nobody had touched.
#
# Pinning the checkout to LF makes every working tree match what is stored,
# on every machine, whatever each developer's core.autocrlf happens to be.
# This is the half that .vscode/settings.json cannot do: files.eol governs
# files the editor creates, and this governs files Git writes.
#
# text=auto still lets Git detect binaries, so the audio fixtures under
# backend/tests/fixtures/ are left exactly as they are.
* text=auto eol=lf
